WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.287
Third, DNS security: two layers in series.

2
00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:08.290
The agency's own resolvers
apply the agency's policy,

3
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:13.037
response policy zones or a DNS
firewall, with every hit logged.

4
00:00:13.037 --> 00:00:17.921
Then all egress recursion goes upstream
through CISA's Protective DNS,

5
00:00:17.921 --> 00:00:23.049
which blocks, redirects or sinkholes
queries that match threat indicators.

6
00:00:23.049 --> 00:00:26.393
So the platform has to forward
to it, preferably encrypted,

7
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:30.363
and that's worth testing: CISA's
own guidance notes that some vendors

8
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:34.263
don't support the protocols for
forwarding queries to its resolver.

9
00:00:34.263 --> 00:00:36.363
Order matters more than presence.

10
00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:40.285
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's Decision

11
00:00:40.285 --> 00:00:43.875
2 is that the agency resolver
is always the first hop.

12
00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:49.354
If the agency also uses a SASE provider's
DNS service, that service is a forwarding

13
00:00:49.354 --> 00:00:54.350
target of the agency resolver, never
the place the branch edge redirects to.

14
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.449
Every component present,
in the wrong order,

15
00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:02.012
quietly removes the agency's own
policy, validation and logging.

16
00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:04.663
Encrypted DNS completes it.

17
00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:10.501
M-22-09 requires encrypted DNS wherever
technically supported, with endpoints

18
00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:16.037
explicitly configured to agency-designated
resolvers rather than ones they discover.

19
00:01:16.037 --> 00:01:19.928
So the platform must
terminate DNS over HTTPS

20
00:01:19.928 --> 00:01:23.512
or TLS on its resolvers
and use it upstream.

21
00:01:23.512 --> 00:01:26.092
And clients mustn't route around it:

22
00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:32.851
TIC 3.0 includes filtering that blocks
unauthorized DNS over HTTPS resolvers.

23
00:01:32.851 --> 00:01:34.650
One product detail to check.

24
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.788
Endpoint DNS agents commonly listen
on the device's loopback address.

25
00:01:39.788 --> 00:01:44.009
They protect a roaming laptop, but an
SD-WAN edge can't redirect to them,

26
00:01:44.009 --> 00:01:47.780
so they don't replace a
network-addressable branch resolver.
